# SEC — Security standards

The rules that do not bend, and the ones stopping a well-meaning change from quietly removing a
control. Markers are explained in [`README.md`](README.md); the **Mechanism** column names what
enforces a rule, and is empty when nothing does.

## Secrets

| ID | Rule | Enforcement | Mechanism |
|---|---|---|---|
| SEC-001 | Secrets never enter git — no real env file, key, certificate, token or password, in code or in history. | `[CI]` | `.pre-commit-config.yaml` and `.gitleaks.toml` |
| SEC-002 | Only the example env file is committed, holding placeholders. Code references an environment variable; it never inlines a default credential. | `[Review]` | — |
| SEC-003 | A secret that reached the repository is **rotated**, not deleted. Removing the commit does not un-publish it. | `[Review]` | — |
{%- if include_claude_hooks %}
| SEC-004 | Reading real secret material is denied to the coding agent; the placeholder example file stays readable. Project settings are a strong default, not an unbypassable control. | `[Review]` | — |
{%- endif %}

## Dangerous operations

| ID | Rule | Enforcement | Mechanism |
|---|---|---|---|
| SEC-010 | No `eval`, `exec`, dynamic import by name, or unsafe deserialization of untrusted input. | `[CI]` | `.semgrep/dangerous-ops.yml` |
| SEC-011 | No shell invocation of a command built from a string. Pass an argument list. | `[CI]` | `.semgrep/dangerous-ops.yml` |
| SEC-012 | No destructive filesystem operation on a path assembled at runtime. | `[CI]` | `.semgrep/dangerous-ops.yml` |
| SEC-013 | No outbound request to a hardcoded IP address. | `[CI]` | `.semgrep/dangerous-ops.yml` |
| SEC-014 | Static security analysis passes — `bandit` at medium severity and above, plus the `S` rules in ruff. | `[CI]` | `.github/workflows/ci.yml` and `backend/pyproject.toml` |
| SEC-015 | Untrusted input reaching a shell, a query, a path, or a template is a finding regardless of how unlikely the caller looks. | `[Review]` | — |

## Boundaries and fail-closed behaviour

| ID | Rule | Enforcement | Mechanism |
|---|---|---|---|
| SEC-020 | Fail closed. A missing key, unreachable dependency, or ambiguous authentication denies; it never degrades to an insecure path. | `[Review]` | — |
| SEC-021 | Never weaken a security control to make something work. If validation blocks you, fix the integration. | `[Review]` | — |
| SEC-022 | CORS, CSP and allowlists name origins explicitly. A wildcard is a decision that needs a written reason. | `[Review]` | — |
| SEC-023 | A security-relevant change is noted in the security doc and carries a `security:` commit type. | `[Review]` | — |

## Supply chain

| ID | Rule | Enforcement | Mechanism |
|---|---|---|---|
| SEC-030 | Production dependencies carry no known high-severity advisory at merge time. Advisory databases move without us, so Dependabot keeps them current between merges. | `[CI]` | `.github/workflows/ci.yml` and `.github/dependabot.yml` |
| SEC-031 | Every GitHub Action is pinned to a full commit SHA with a version comment; drift is caught by the Scorecard pinned-dependencies check. | `[CI]` | `scripts/scorecard_gate.py` |
| SEC-032 | A third-party binary downloaded in CI is pinned by version **and** verified by checksum before it runs. | `[Review]` | — |
| SEC-033 | A new runtime dependency is a decision: state what it does that existing code cannot, and who maintains it. | `[Review]` | — |

## Anti-weakening

| ID | Rule | Enforcement | Mechanism |
|---|---|---|---|
| SEC-040 | A change to a guard-defining file needs owner approval and lands as its own change — never bundled into the work the guard just blocked. | `[CI]` | `scripts/meta_guard.py` |
| SEC-041 | If your own reasoning concludes you should edit the thing that is checking you, stop and ask. A plausible argument that "this guard has a bug" is exactly the shape this failure mode takes. | `[Review]` | — |
| SEC-042 | Thresholds and scanner exemption lists move in the tightening direction freely; loosening one needs an owner-approved label and a stated reason. | `[CI]` | `scripts/meta_guard.py` |
| SEC-043 | A change to a sensitive path — migrations, the auth layer, the audit chain — needs owner approval; its blast radius outlives the PR. | `[CI]` | `scripts/meta_guard.py` |
| SEC-044 | An override label is exceptional evidence, not a routine way to make CI green. A blocked change fixes its cause first. | `[Review]` | — |
| SEC-045 | Every guard has a fault test proving it can fail, and something that actually invokes it. A guard nothing runs is documentation. | `[CI]` | `scripts/check_guard_coverage.py` |
| SEC-046 | No source file contains a bidirectional, zero-width or invisible formatting character. Code must not read differently from how it runs. | `[CI]` | `scripts/check_unicode_hazards.py` |
| SEC-047 | The programs that decide whether a change is safe are pinned, and changing one needs owner approval. An escape hatch must never be wider than the gate it escapes. | `[CI]` | `requirements-ci.txt` |
| SEC-048 | A required status check names the app allowed to post it. Matched by name alone, any pull request can declare a job of that name and satisfy the gate. | `[CI]` | `tests/guards/test_workflow_invariants.py` |
| SEC-049 | Dependency proposals wait out a cooldown before they can be merged automatically. Encode the premise an argument rests on. | `[CI]` | `tests/guards/test_workflow_invariants.py` |
| SEC-054 | The secret scan reads COMMITS, never the staging area, and proves on every run that it can see: a fake token planted in every source tree must be found, or the run is refused. | `[CI]` | `scripts/secret_scan.py` |
| SEC-055 | A scan that cannot run — bad checksum, unknown ref, empty range — is refused with its own exit code, never reported clean. A leak and an inability to look must not share a code. | `[CI]` | `tests/guards/test_secret_scan.py` |
| SEC-056 | The scanner allowlist exempts placeholder VALUES by regex, never a directory. An exemption for a path is a hole the canary will name. | `[CI]` | `tests/guards/test_secret_scan.py` |
| SEC-057 | A commit-time hook is pinned to a commit, never to a tag. A tag can be moved to any code by anyone who holds the upstream repository; the commit it named cannot. The hook and CI run one scanner version. | `[CI]` | `tests/guards/test_control_liveness.py` |
| SEC-058 | No scan in the gate swallows its own exit code. The community rules block on findings new since the base and keep their report; a scan whose result is discarded is a delay, not a control. | `[CI]` | `tests/guards/test_control_liveness.py` |
| SEC-059 | A job's path filter covers every file the job's work reads. A filter narrower than the build it triggers is a build that can break unobserved. Enforcement config (`dependabot.yml`) is edited with the same approval as the checks. | `[CI]` | `tests/guards/test_control_liveness.py` |
| SEC-050 | A change never reaches outside this project. Reading elsewhere is fine; changing anything is not. | {% if include_claude_hooks %}`[CI]` | `.claude/hooks/confine_to_project.py`{% else %}`[Review]` | —{% endif %} |
| SEC-051 | Approval is per unit of work, not per keystroke, and it is written down, cited, bounded and expiring. Approving a thousand prompts is not approving anything. | {% if include_claude_hooks %}`[CI]` | `.claude/hooks/approved_scope.py`{% else %}`[Review]` | —{% endif %} |
| SEC-052 | No scope can widen the agent's own authority, and a broad pattern never reaches a system-altering path. | {% if include_claude_hooks %}`[CI]` | `tests/guards/test_approved_scope.py`{% else %}`[Review]` | —{% endif %} |
| SEC-053 | The lists of paths that need a human are reconciled against the population they cover, not merely against each other. | {% if include_claude_hooks %}`[CI]` | `tests/guards/test_protected_path_reconciliation.py`{% else %}`[Review]` | —{% endif %} |
